# Core §15.5 — THE EXTREME-NUMERIC REFUSALS, PINNED.
#
# §15.5 claims that the closed grammar refuses the extreme-numeric class
# before anything reaches the layout pass, and a security claim may only say
# what a fixture or a gate demonstrates. Two fixtures already carried half of
# it — `361-pin-errors` pins that `at=` takes a parenthesised point, and
# `367-pin-extent-strict-value` pins `width=0`, `width=-50` and `width=1e3`
# as *width must be a positive number (px)*. What neither carried is the
# INSIDE of the parens: the values an attacker or a confused generator
# actually reaches for once it has learned the `at=(x,y)` shape.
#
# So this file writes them. `1e12` is scientific notation, which the point
# grammar does not admit at all; `NaN` and `Infinity` are the two IEEE
# non-finite spellings that a naive `parseFloat` accepts and that would reach
# geometry as a coordinate no viewBox can hold. All three get the SAME
# message as a malformed point, and that is the finding worth pinning: the
# grammar does not have a special case for them, it simply never had a
# production they could match. §15.5 says "none of this is a security feature
# that was designed" — this golden is what makes that sentence checkable.
#
# THE COMPLEMENT, and it is why §15.5 does not overclaim: a LARGE PLAIN
# DECIMAL is accepted. `pin at=(999999999999999999999,0)` renders, to a
# document whose viewBox is `0 0 1e+21 76`. That is not written here because
# it is not an error — it is the unbounded-canvas half of §15.5, which is
# UNSPECIFIED and has no gate by construction. The refusals below are a
# grammar boundary, not a magnitude limit, and a reader who mistook one for
# the other would trust a bound that does not exist.
figdown 0.4 block
node a "A"
node b "B"
layout
pin a at=(1e12,1e12)
pin b at=(NaN,NaN)
pin a at=(Infinity,0)
pin b at=(-1e12,-1e12)
pin a at=(1e400,0)
